  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mainly dry, tranquil and cooler weather pattern \nexpected into the last weekend of July. \n\nSky is clearing nicely now after a bit of a struggle to get rid\nof the thick blanket of cu across the north. Slightly higher\nelev clouds over the SE are also headed out to the east as the\nupper trough axis slowly shifts overhead. Fog still looks\nprobable in the deep river valleys of the N. \n\nPrevious...\n\nHigh pressure continues to build in with a refreshingly dry\nairmass on the way. Evening stratocumulus and cumulus will fall\napart/erode after sunset giving way to mainly clear skies\novernight. With dewpoints dropping into the 40s and 50s tonight,\nclear skies and calm winds will allow temperatures to plummet\nto well below normal for this time of year. Low temperatures by\nThursday morning will range from near 40 in the northwest to the\nmid 50s in the SE. With river temperatures much warmer than the\nincoming airmass, radiational cooling will support areas of \nvalley fog late tonight into early Thursday morning, especially\nin the deepest valleys of northwest Pennsylvania.\n\nHigh pressure will gradually drift across Pennsylvania over the\nnext few days, ensuring a sustained period of cooler than\naverage and dry/less-humid weather. The center of high pressure\nwill pass east of Pennsylvania later this week, resulting in\nwinds shifting around to come out of the south. As such,\ntemperatures and humidity will begin to increase once again. An\nupper trough carving out over the eastern Great Lakes into the \nNortheast US will bring rain chances back into the forecast \nSunday into next week. At this time, no significant systems are\nanticipated, but long range outlooks favor temperatures\nremaining near or below average through the end of July.",
